{"code":0,"data":{"title":"Hands-On Linear Regression Training with PyTorch","content":"# Hands-On Linear Regression Training with PyTorch\n\nBased on Jupyter Notebook examples from my own experiments, this post uses **PyTorch** to **build, train, and understand a linear regression model** from scratch. It is aimed at beginners, and also helps you form an accurate mental model of PyTorch's autograd and training loop.\n\n## 1. Recap: the linear regression problem[ #](#1-recap-the-linear-regression-problem)\n\nLinear regression is the most basic supervised learning model. Its mathematical form is:\n\ny^​=wx+b\n\nwhere:\n\n* x: input feature\n* w: weight\n* b: bias\n* y^​: predicted value\n\nThe goal of training is to learn suitable w, b from the data so that the prediction y^​ is as close as possible to the true value y.\n\n## 2. PyTorch basics: Tensors and autograd[ #](#2-pytorch-basics-tensors-and-autograd)\n\n### What is a Tensor[ #](#what-is-a-tensor)\n\nIn a Jupyter Notebook environment, install torch first, then create a sample tensor:\n\n![Creating a simple tensor](/post/introtopytorchlinearregr/a571579617c9.png)\n\nThis is the simplest kind of Tensor: it has no gradient and is constructed from a matrix-like array. A Tensor has several important attributes, including `shape`, `dtype`, and `device`.\n\n* `shape`: the shape of the matrix, i.e. how many rows and columns.\n* `dtype`: the data type of the elements.\n* `device`: the device the tensor lives on, typically `cpu` or `cuda`.\n\n> A `device` of `cuda` enables NVIDIA's CUDA, running computation on the GPU, which can significantly speed up tensor operations.\n\nExample:\n\n![Tensor shape, dtype and device](/post/introtopytorchlinearregr/ffbe12c0bdd3.png)\n\nSo a Tensor is essentially a wrapper around matrix data. Let's explore its other features.\n\n### Autograd on Tensors[ #](#autograd-on-tensors)\n\nHaving looked at **creating Tensors and their attributes**, next comes a parameter you will use in any ML/LLM work: `requires_grad`. It is the core of creating tensors that support automatic differentiation:\n\n![Tensor with requires\\_grad](/post/introtopytorchlinearregr/521dc31c8d4e.png)\n\n`requires_grad` means that computations involving the Tensor are tracked, so that you can later differentiate with respect to it (compute gradients).\n\nIn the example below, we first run a series of computations on Tensors:\n\n![Computations on tensors](/post/introtopytorchlinearregr/6f168e362648.png)\n\nYou can see that the Tensors `a`, `b`, `c`, and `d` all have a `grad_fn` attribute, which indicates the operation that produced that Tensor.\n\n### Backpropagation on Tensors[ #](#backpropagation-on-tensors)\n\nTensor backpropagation provides the ability to trace computations, but `backward()` can only be called directly on a scalar.\n\n![Gradient of a scalar](/post/introtopytorchlinearregr/55f6edf4d802.png)\n\nAs shown above, the scalar `x` goes through a series of computations, and in the end its gradient (slope) can be read from the `.grad` attribute.\n\n> Besides what is covered here, Tensors also support median, max, sum, and much more; see the [official documentation](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/torch.html) for details.\n\n## 3. Building a linear layer with `torch.nn.Linear`[ #](#3-building-a-linear-layer-with-torchnnlinear)\n\nHere we use the linear model from torch's Neural Network module; the core is building a basic linear layer:\n\npythonCopy\n\n```python\nlinear_layer = torch.nn.Linear(in_features=1, out_features=1)\n\nprint(linear_layer.weight)\nprint(linear_layer.bias)\n```\n\nThis step is equivalent to defining the formula:\n\ny=wx+b\n\nwhere:\n\n* `weight` corresponds to w\n* `bias` corresponds to b\n\n## 4. Defining the linear regression model[ #](#4-defining-the-linear-regression-model)\n\nDefining the linear regression model takes several steps.\n\n### Step 1: Prepare the data[ #](#step-1-prepare-the-data)\n\nThe data here generates `X` in the formula with random numbers, then applies the basic computation plus a bit of random noise to simulate a real-world environment.\n\n![Generating regression data](/post/introtopytorchlinearregr/8d73bda6f09c.png)\n\nHere `true_W` and `true_b` are the ground-truth values and `y_true` is the simulated output. Through training, we want to find `W` and `b` close to the ground truth.\n\n> In a real scenario, `X` might be an instrument's input (the independent variable), and `y_true` the historically recorded results for each `X` (the dependent variable). This data is what the model is trained on, and in practice it would be much larger.\n\n### Step 2: Define the regression model[ #](#step-2-define-the-regression-model)\n\nThere is more than one way to implement this. Although defining a model by subclassing `torch.nn.Module` is generally recommended, to illustrate what happens inside, I use a functional style here and compute the parameter updates by hand.\n\n![Functional regression training loop](/post/introtopytorchlinearregr/a76db5f8343b.png)\n\nJust this one loop is all it takes to train the model.\n\n## 5. Forward pass[ #](#5-forward-pass)\n\n### What is the forward pass[ #](#what-is-the-forward-pass)\n\nThe forward pass feeds input data through the model to produce predictions.\n\nwasmCopy\n\n```wasm\ninput data → model → predicted result\n```\n\n(That is: input data → model → predictions.)\n\n> The model here starts out inaccurate, so its outputs may be wrong; the parameters need further adjustment to improve accuracy.\n\n### The corresponding code[ #](#the-corresponding-code)\n\nHere is the code:\n\npythonCopy\n\n```python\n# forward pass and loss\ny_hat = X @ W + b\n```\n\nPyTorch automatically builds the computation graph here and records the relationships between operations (see the Tensor section above).\n\n## 6. Defining the loss function[ #](#6-defining-the-loss-function)\n\nThe most common loss for linear regression is **mean squared error (MSE)**:\n\npythonCopy\n\n```python\nloss = torch.mean((y_hat - y_true) ** 2)\n```\n\nMathematically:\n\nMSE=N1​∑(y−y^​)2\n\nThe loss measures how good the model's predictions are; the smaller, the better.\n\n## 7. Backward pass[ #](#7-backward-pass)\n\n### What is the backward pass[ #](#what-is-the-backward-pass)\n\nDuring the forward pass, a computation graph is built and the relationships between operations are recorded. The backward pass propagates in the opposite direction along that graph, computing the gradients of every parameter with `requires_grad=True`.\n\n### The corresponding code[ #](#the-corresponding-code-1)\n\nThe backward pass is just one line:\n\npythonCopy\n\n```python\n# backward pass\nloss.backward()\n```\n\nWith this single call, we can compute the gradients of our two parameters, `W` and `b`.\n\n## 8. Updating the parameters[ #](#8-updating-the-parameters)\n\nThe core snippet for updating parameters:\n\npythonCopy\n\n```python\n# update parameters\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    W -= learning_rate * W.grad\n    b -= learning_rate * b.grad\n\n# zero gradients\nW.grad.zero_()\nb.grad.zero_()\n```\n\nHere we nudge the parameters to reduce the loss above, so that `W` and `b` approach their ground-truth values.\n\nTwo things to note:\n\n* So that adjusting `W` and `b` does not get recorded in the computation graph, we use `torch.no_grad`; inside it, tensor operations are not recorded in the graph.\n* After updating the parameters, we need to reset the gradients of `W` and `b`; otherwise they accumulate into the next iteration and cause confusion.\n\n## 9. A more idiomatic approach[ #](#9-a-more-idiomatic-approach)\n\nIn real-world settings, training code is not usually written like this. With two parameters you can do it by hand, but LLMs have 7B, 256B, or more parameters, so writing it this way is completely impractical. torch already provides ready-made implementations of the manual computation above, and much more besides. Below is a more general approach (defining the model by subclassing `torch.nn.Module` and using torch's built-in machinery).\n\n![Regression model with nn.Module](/post/introtopytorchlinearregr/c28cf58dab1a.png)\n\nBecause torch already defines the common models, optimizers, and loss functions, following this conventional style hides all of the parameter-update logic inside the optimizer, which is very clean.\n\n## 10. Summary[ #](#10-summary)\n\nThrough the hands-on example in this post, we started from **the most basic mathematical model**, walked through the entire process of training a linear regression model in PyTorch, and gradually opened up the \"black box\" of PyTorch's autograd and training mechanics.\n\nThe key takeaways:\n\n1. **Linear regression is not complicated at its core** Whether it is the mathematical formula\n\n   y=wx+b\n\n   or PyTorch's `nn.Linear`, both are essentially an engineered wrapper around this one formula. Understanding that helps you keep your \"model intuition\" as you move on to more complex models.\n\n2. **The Tensor is PyTorch's core data structure** A Tensor is more than a \"multi-dimensional array\": with `requires_grad=True`, it becomes a node in the computation graph and automatically participates in gradient computation, which is the foundation of PyTorch's efficient training.\n\n3. **Forward and backward form the training loop**\n\n   * Forward pass: compute predictions and loss from the current parameters\n   * Backward pass: compute gradients by propagating the loss backward\n   * Parameter update: use the gradients to update the parameters\n\n   This flow applies not only to linear regression but to almost every deep learning model.\n\n4. **Understanding a hand-written training loop matters** Although in practice we usually use:\n\n   * `nn.Module`\n   * `torch.optim`\n   * `nn.MSELoss`\n\n   implementing parameter updates and gradient zeroing by hand lets you truly understand:\n\n   * where gradients come from\n   * why you need `zero_grad()`\n   * why parameter updates belong inside `torch.no_grad()`\n\n5. **High-level abstractions are built on low-level principles** PyTorch's high-level APIs are not \"magic\"; they are a high-level abstraction over these basic steps. Once you understand the underlying logic, the standard style turns out to be both elegant and reliable.\n\nOverall, **linear regression is the best starting point for learning PyTorch and deep learning**. If you truly understand every step in this post, then multi-layer networks, CNNs, RNNs, and today's popular large language models (LLMs) all share the same training lineage.\n\nDirections you can try next:\n\n* Use real datasets (e.g. house prices, time series)\n* Introduce `Dataset` / `DataLoader`\n* Move the model to the GPU (CUDA)\n* Extend to multi-dimensional features or multi-layer networks\n\nUnderstand the fundamentals, and you can go much further.\n","locale":"en","slug":"introtopytorchlinearregr"}}